# The proof editor, vim-vt510, on lines of every size. The editor holds
# the text in memory and wraps each line itself, as zsh and vim do, so
# text flows onto the next row when you insert and flows back when you
# delete. The typing pad (typepad.py) of the insert and double-size guides
# shows the opposite: what the terminal does on its own, where nothing
# moves to another row. The editor's sample file has a section for each
# step. Each step copies the sample to /tmp/vim-vt510.txt and opens the
# copy, so a step starts from the sample and never changes the checkout.
# make vt-check runs the guide from the checkout's top directory, so the
# steps name the editor by that relative path.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=editor

title: the proof editor, which wraps lines itself

intro: Each step starts vim-vt510, a small editor, on a copy of its sample
intro: file. The editor holds the text and wraps each line itself, as zsh and
intro: vim do, and redraws the rows after each key. So text flows onto the
intro: next row when you insert, and back when you delete. The typing pad of
intro: the insert guide shows the terminal on its own, where nothing moves to
intro: another row. The status line near the bottom, in reverse video, shows
intro: the file, the mode, and the cursor's line and column. i starts
intro: inserting, Esc stops, :q quits and :q! quits without saving. Steps
intro: whose titles start with "Explore" have no right or wrong answer and
intro: ask only for a note.

step: Text flows onto the next row when you insert
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press Down five times, onto the line that starts "A long normal line".
do: Press Right about twenty times, then i, and type a few words.
expect: Each letter goes in at the cursor. The text after it moves right,
expect: and the letters at the end of each row move to the start of the next
expect: row. The status line and the bottom row stay where they are.
expect: Wrong: a letter lost at the right edge, or text over the status line.

step: Text flows back when you delete
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press Down five times, then i, and type a few words. Press Backspace
do: until the words are gone. Then press Esc and x a few times.
expect: The text moves back left after each key. The letters at the start of
expect: each continuation row move up to the end of the row above, so the
expect: rows stay full.
expect: Wrong: a gap in a row, a doubled letter, or a row that stays short.

step: A line made wide or tall wraps at half the width
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press Down five times. Type :wide and Enter, then :tall and Enter,
do: then :normal and Enter.
expect: After :wide, the line's letters are twice as wide. The line wraps
expect: in the middle of the screen and takes twice as many rows. After
expect: :tall, each of those rows is a pair of rows that shows the top and
expect: bottom halves of the same letters. The lines below move down. After
expect: :normal, the line is back as it was.
expect: Wrong: a row of the line at another size, or a tall letter whose two
expect: halves do not match.

step: Typing past the edge of a tall line starts a second pair
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press Down nine times, onto "A tall line". Press a, then End, and type
do: until the text passes the middle of the screen.
expect: The letters past the middle start a new tall pair below, and every
expect: tall letter is whole. The lines below move down to make room.
expect: Wrong: a letter lost at the edge, or a row of normal size in the line.

step: Wide characters move to the next row whole
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press PageDown, then Up or Down until the cursor is on the line that
do: starts "Wide characters". Press i and type letters until a wide
do: character reaches the end of a row.
expect: A wide character that no longer fits at the end of a row moves to
expect: the start of the next row whole, and leaves the last column empty.
expect: Wrong: half of a wide character at the end of a row.

step: Saving keeps each line's size
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Press Down five times and type :wide and Enter. Type :q and Enter.
do: Then type :w and Enter, :e /tmp/vim-vt510.txt and Enter, and :q and
do: Enter.
expect: The first :q refuses to quit: the bottom row says the file has
expect: unsaved changes. :w saves, and :e reads the file again. After :e,
expect: each line has the size it had, the line you made wide too. The last
expect: :q quits.
expect: Wrong: a line at another size after :e, or a quit with unsaved
expect: changes.

step: Explore the editor
run: cp tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510-sample.txt /tmp/vim-vt510.txt && python3 tools/vt/checks/vim-vt510.py /tmp/vim-vt510.txt
do: Edit as you like: mix sizes with :wide, :tall and :normal, split and
do: join lines with Enter and Backspace, and open new lines with o.
expect: There is no right or wrong here. Note anything that surprised you.
